        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Meroë</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 591 BCE – c. 350 CE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Founded</span><span class="info-stat-val">King Aspelta moved the Kushite capital to Meroë, possibly c. 591 BCE, just after the sack of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napata">Napata</a> by the Egyptian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psamtik-ii">Psamtik II</a>; the city is said to have borne the older name Saba.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where</span><span class="info-stat-val">On the east bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>, about 6 km north-east of the Kabushiya station and 35 km north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shendi">Shendi</a>, about 200 km north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khartoum">Khartoum</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">Capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushite-empire">Kingdom of Kush</a>; the pyramids of its kings and queens; an iron industry; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meroitic-script">Meroitic script</a>; the war and peace of 22 BCE with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandake">Kandake</a>, or Queen Mother; its end in the 4th century CE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Today</span><span class="info-stat-val">Meroë is an archaeological site near the villages of Bagrawiyah in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>, listed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> in 2011.</span></div>
