    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Meroë became the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushite-empire">Kingdom of Kush</a> around 590 BCE, when King Aspelta moved the seat of government south from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napata">Napata</a>, and it remained the royal city for several centuries.</li><li>Its rulers drew wealth from iron, pottery, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and trade in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, gold and other goods, and from the 3rd century BCE they were buried in the pyramids at Meroë, which numbered more than two hundred.</li><li>After war with Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and the decline of its trade, Meroë faded as a power from the 1st or 2nd century CE, and in about 350 CE it was destroyed by Axum, though some scholars point to a decline already around 300.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>A head of the emperor Augustus, looted from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubians">Nubians</a>, was buried under the steps of a temple at Meroë. It is now kept in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

