    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 591 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">King Aspelta moves the Kushite capital to Meroë after the sack of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napata">Napata</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 280 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The royal burial ground is transferred from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napata">Napata</a> to Meroë from the reign of Arakamani</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">23 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The Roman governor Petronius invades <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> in answer to a Nubian attack on southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">22 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and Meroë sign a peace treaty under Augustus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amanirenas">Amanirenas</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">61</span><span class="rf-text">A party sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a> reaches the city of Meroë</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">350</span><span class="rf-text">Meroë is destroyed by Axum</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/psamtik-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/psamtik-ii.jpg" alt="Psamtik II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/psamtik-ii">Psamtik II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_Psamtik_II_Louvre.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OliBac, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Meroë stood on the east bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> on the edge of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/butana">Butana</a>, the region bounded by the Nile, the Atbarah and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blue-nile">Blue Nile</a>, which took its name as the Island of Meroë from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushite-empire">Kushite kingdom</a>. The Kushite kings chose the site, between the Fifth and Sixth Cataracts, because it lay on the fringe of the summer rainfall belt, in an area rich in iron ore and hardwood for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-technology">ironworking</a>, with access to trade routes to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a>.</p><p>The kingdom's first main capital was farther north at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napata">Napata</a>. King Aspelta moved the capital to Meroë, possibly c. 591 BCE, just after the sack of Napata by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psamtik-ii">Psamtik II</a>. One account holds that the city originally bore the name Saba, after the country's original founder, and that the Persian king Cambyses gave it the name Meroë in honour of his sister. Partially deciphered Meroitic texts give its native name as Medewi.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The importance of the town increased gradually from the beginning of the Meroitic Period, especially from the reign of Arakamani, c. 280 BCE, when the royal burial ground moved to Meroë from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napata">Napata</a>. Excavations had already found high-ranking Kushite burials of the Napatan Period in the Western Cemetery near the settlement. During the third Meroitic period, which began in 270 BCE, Meroë flourished and many building projects were undertaken. At its peak its rulers controlled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-valley">Nile Valley</a> over a straight-line distance of more than 1,000 km.</p><p>The kingdom's wealth was centred on a strong iron industry, and metalworking is believed to have taken place at Meroë. Pottery was also a widespread industry, and fine, elaborately decorated wares carried social significance and are believed to have been used in mortuary rites. Meroë traded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, slaves, rare skins, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrich-feather">ostrich feathers</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebony">ebony</a>, and exported textiles and jewellery. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">Cotton</a> working reached its highest achievement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> around 400 BCE, and Nubia was rich in gold. The sakia was used to raise water for irrigation. After the war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> the period was marked by abundant trade finds at sites in Meroë.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kandake"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kandake.jpg" alt="Kandake" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kandake">Kandake</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aegyptisches_Museum_Berlin_InvNr7261_20080313_Barkenuntersatz_Natakamani_Amanitore_aus_Wad_Ban_Naga_4.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sven-Steffen Arndt, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Meroë was the southern capital of a kingdom that spanned c. 800 BCE to c. 350 CE. In the first Meroitic period (542 to 315 BCE) the kings ruled over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napata">Napata</a> and Meroë; the seat of government and the royal palace were at Meroë, the main temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a> at Napata, and kings and many queens were buried at Nuri, with some queens buried in the West Cemetery at Meroë. The earliest king was Analmaye (542 to 538 BCE) and the last of the phase Nastasen (335 to 315 BCE).</p><p>In the second period, of the early 3rd century BCE, kings and many queens were buried at Meroë in the South Cemetery, and in the third period (270 BCE to the 1st century CE) kings were buried in the North Cemetery and queens in the West Cemetery. The first king of that period was Arakamani and the last ruler Queen Amanitore. In the fourth period, from the 1st to the 4th century CE, the first king was Shorkaror, and the last rulers may have been Yesebokheamani or Talakhidamani.</p><p>The king was an autocratic ruler who shared his authority only with the Queen Mother, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandake">Kandake</a>, whose role remains obscure. The administration included treasurers, seal bearers, heads of archives and chief scribes.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/herodotus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/herodotus.jpg" alt="Herodotus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marble_bust_of_Herodotos_MET_DT11742_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Greek historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> described Meroë in the 5th century BCE as a great city said to be the mother city of the other Ethiopians. Its people worshipped southern deities such as Apedemak, the lion son of Sekhmet (or of Bast, depending on the region), and also continued to worship Egyptian gods they had brought with them, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a>, Tefnut, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horus">Horus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isis">Isis</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thoth">Thoth</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sati-practice">Satis</a>, though to a lesser extent.</p><p>The Meroitic language was spoken at Meroë and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> from at least 300 BCE and became extinct around 400 CE. It was written in two forms of the Meroitic alphabet: Meroitic Cursive, written with a stylus for general record keeping, and Meroitic Hieroglyphic, carved in stone or used for royal or religious documents. The script, of twenty-three letters, was derived from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptian-hieroglyphs">Egyptian hieroglyphs</a> and replaced the Egyptian script by the 3rd century BCE. It is not well understood, because few bilingual texts survive, and scholars disagree whether the language belonged to the Afro-Asiatic or the Nilo-Saharan family. The Meroitic settlements of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/butana">Butana</a> lived by exploiting rainlands and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savannah">savannah</a>, and power was associated with control of people rather than of territory.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/nero"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nero.jpg" alt="Nero" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nero_Glyptothek_Munich_321.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> led to border skirmishes and Meroitic incursions beyond the Roman frontier. In 23 BCE, in response to a Nubian attack on southern Egypt, the Roman governor Publius Petronius invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>, pillaged northern Nubia and sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napata">Napata</a> in 22 BCE before returning home. In retaliation the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubians">Nubians</a> crossed the lower border of Egypt and looted statues from the towns near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-cataract">First Cataract</a> at Aswan. Rome later reclaimed some of them, and others were returned after the peace treaty of 22 BCE between Augustus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amanirenas">Amanirenas</a>.</p><p>In the autumn of 61 CE the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a> sent Praetorian soldiers under a tribune and two centurions into the country. They reached Meroë, were given an escort and went up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-nile">White Nile</a> as far as the swamps of the Sudd, the limit of Roman penetration into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>.</p><p>The kingdom began to fade as a power by the 1st or 2nd century CE, sapped by the war with Roman Egypt and the decline of its traditional industries. The collapse of its external trade with other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-valley">Nile Valley</a> states may have been a prime cause of the decline of royal power in the 3rd and 4th centuries. In 350 CE Meroë was destroyed by Axum, and the Aksumite presence was short lived before Meroë was taken by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alodia">Kingdom of Alodia</a>. A stele of an Aksumite king, thought to be <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ezana">Ezana</a>, was found at the site. Some authorities read such inscriptions as proof that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire">Aksumites</a> destroyed Kush, while others note that archaeology points to an economic and political decline around 300.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The site is marked by more than two hundred pyramids in three groups, many of them in ruins, with the distinctive size and proportions of Nubian pyramids. They were built over burial chambers holding bodies that had been burned or buried without mummification, and the chapel walls carried reliefs of queens and some kings with chapters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-the-dead">Book of the Dead</a>. Two other Meroitic cities, Musawwarat es-Sufra and Naqa, lay in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/butana">Butana</a>.</p><p>The site became known to Europeans in 1821 through the French mineralogist Frédéric Cailliaud. Giuseppe Ferlini carried out small excavations in 1834, C. R. Lepsius examined the ruins in 1844, E. A. Wallis Budge excavated in 1902 and 1905, and in 1910 J. Garstang began excavations for the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a> that found a palace and several temples. The best of the chapel reliefs were taken down in 1905 and set up in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a> and the museum at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khartoum">Khartoum</a>. Jewish oral tradition holds that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a>, in his youth, led an Egyptian expedition to Meroë, then called Saba.</p><p>The ruins lie near the villages of Bagrawiyah, and in June 2011 the sites of Meroë were listed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Sites</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Meroë, on the east bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>, became the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushite-empire">Kingdom of Kush</a> around 590 BCE. Its kings and queens were buried there in more than two hundred pyramids, and its iron and trade made it rich. It was destroyed in about 350 CE and is now a World Heritage site.</em></p>

